WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.649
This is a little piece of jazz history and
it's one of the coolest things I've ever held.

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:10.980
It's three cropped photos held together with
scotch tape and a note dictating how to print

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.490
the negatives for the final photos.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:18.840
The remarkable thing about this post-it note-sized
image, is how little it changed when it became

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:27.640
the full album cover for jazz saxophonist
Joe Henderson's 1966 classic "Mode for Joe."

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:34.190
The story of how this became this didn't start
in 1966.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:40.840
It started in 1939, when two Jewish-German
immigrants, Francis Wolff and Alfred Lion,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.500
started the Blue Note jazz label in New York
City that would come to be revered not only

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.320
for its sound, but for its iconic album covers.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.399
What they were going for is, they wanted to
make tunes that were memorable, that people

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:06.510
could walk out of a club humming, and that
had a certain amount of soul to them.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.120
That's Michael Cuscuna, a record producer
and Blue Note aficionado.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.230
That soul sound Michael's describing was called
Hard Bop, a genre of jazz steeped in Gospel

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:21.680
and Rhythm &amp; Blues that became synonymous
with Blue Note by the mid-1950s.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.580
The  mastermind behind the sound was recording

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.990
engineer Rudy Van Gelder.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.000
"I'm not standing too close when we play the
ensemble am I?

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.270
You want me to articulate my solo?

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.360
Yeah, you're a little too close.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.090
Yeah well I'll step back a little bit.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.380
Is that it?

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.390
Uh, this is take four.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:54.240
When you heard a Blue Note record, you heard
a lot of air coming through the saxophones

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.550
and the trumpets.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:59.660
And you heard all the power and crystal details
of the drums.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:05.040
That description is fully evident in Art Blakey
and the Jazz Messengers' "The Drum Thunder Suite."

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:26.640
That was kind of the classic time for modern
jazz and New York was heaven on Earth.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:32.040
There was a record store on every other block,
throughout all of, not midtown, but also the

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.360
Village and Harlem.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.780
And, if you went to those record stores, it
probably wasn't the sound of Blue Note that

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:40.760
immediately caught your attention — it was
their album covers.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:51.950
Their bold use of color, intimate photography,
and meticulous placed typography came to define

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.750
the look of jazz during the 1950s and '60s.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:01.500
These covers are energetic, moody, and they're
sophisticated.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.620
These album covers are jazz.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.480
Now, if you flip a Blue Note record over,
it's likely you'll see two names credited

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.010
for its look.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:16.990
Cover Photo, Francis Wolff, Cover Design,
Reid Miles.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:21.310
Francis Wolff started shooting every Blue
Note session the minute he arrived.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:29.230
One of the most impressive and kind of shocking
things was that the average success rate of

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.350
those photos was really extraordinary.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:36.630
He's like the jazz artist of photography in
the sense that he could nail it immediately.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:42.230
But the person who decided that this photo
would be this cover, was graphic designer

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:43.460
Reid Miles.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:48.709
He could look at a contact sheet and zero
in on one image that you or I might not even

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:54.080
think twice about, and find a little crop
of a square that would be like, wow, that's

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.080
a dynamite image.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.720
Yeah, that is dynamite.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.620
One of the things that amazed me was what
I call the "pullback effect."

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.240
Take Hank Mobley's "No Room for Squares."

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.090
There was a new subway station that was built,
it was unlike any other subway stop.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.590
It had these metal concentric circles.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.440
Now, try to find the final album cover.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.300
There it is.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.740
The pullback shows you the whole image and
it shows you an insight into the eye of the

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.870
designer, that I think is absolutely amazing.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.520
This is Lee Morgan's "Search for the New Land."

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.880
What was really going on was, Lee was listening
to the music and sitting next to him is Alfred Lion

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.620
with his eyes closed in a reverie.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.120
Listening to the same music.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:55.300
By the 1960s, Reid became more and more adventurous
in how he'd use typography, sometimes omitting

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.580
photos altogether.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.340
This is Joe Henderson's 1964 album, "In'n
Out."

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:07.270
A tiny crop of a photo of Henderson plays
a supporting role to a bold and energetic

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.880
design, with those arrows driving the feeling
of the album title home.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.150
There's a Jackie McClean album called "It's Time."

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.250
There's that tiny crop of a photo again.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:21.310
And then, just exclamation points for the
rest of this cover.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:22.550
Black type on white.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:27.680
And it's just startlingly beautiful and startlingly
getting your attention.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:34.140
But some of my favorite covers are the ones
where Reid uses Wolff's photography as a playground

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.600
for typography.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:44.160
If you look at the contact sheet of "Uno
Mas," the upper-right hand shot is the one

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.560
that Reid Miles focused on.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.020
That would probably be one of the least memorable
images on a contact sheet.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.180
There's photos of Herbie Hancock and Joe Henderson.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:00.540
And also there's two shots of Tony Williams
who, in this photo, looks 17 years old.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.500
And who is 17 years old.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:09.540
But Reid chose this image and perfectly placed
the title of the album right in Kenny Dorham's

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.540
hand.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.600
He did this with Freddie Roach's "Good Move!"

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:19.340
And fit "Our Man in Paris" around Dexter Gordon's
cigarette.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:26.370
What always amazed me about art directors
was the ones that could create a look for

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:32.960
a record that was highly individual, but also
that fit into a stream that gave the label a look.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.740
Reid Miles was a master of that.

